WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.275
&gt;&gt; I'd like to
welcome Cynthia Dwork

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:12.120
to our Fireside Chat today

00:00:12.121 --> 00:00:15.315
and to your MSR AI
distinguished lecture

00:00:15.316 --> 00:00:16.905
coming up in the next hour.

00:00:16.906 --> 00:00:19.980
Cynthia is the Gordon
McKay professor

00:00:19.981 --> 00:00:21.615
at Harvard University,

00:00:21.616 --> 00:00:23.220
the Radcliffe
alumnae professor at

00:00:23.221 --> 00:00:25.830
the Radcliffe Institute
for Advanced Study,

00:00:25.831 --> 00:00:28.470
and affiliated faculty
at Harvard Law,

00:00:28.471 --> 00:00:29.910
as well as a
distinguished scientist

00:00:29.911 --> 00:00:32.380
at Microsoft Research. Welcome.

00:00:32.381 --> 00:00:33.965
&gt;&gt; Thank you. It's
good to be here.

00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:37.230
&gt;&gt; I wanted to start
off with thinking about

00:00:37.231 --> 00:00:38.730
your background of the breadth of

00:00:38.731 --> 00:00:41.055
topics you've worked on
in Computer Science.

00:00:41.056 --> 00:00:44.200
I wanted to ask you, how you
got into Computer Science,

00:00:44.201 --> 00:00:47.270
and then how you
chose the problems,

00:00:47.271 --> 00:00:48.390
the breadth, the
problems that you've

00:00:48.391 --> 00:00:50.010
worked on from cryptography

00:00:50.011 --> 00:00:54.270
to privacy and now fairness.

00:00:54.271 --> 00:00:58.000
&gt;&gt; So that's a broad question.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.725
I got into Computer Science
initially on a lark.

00:01:02.726 --> 00:01:07.120
When I was applying to
college my mother said,

00:01:07.121 --> 00:01:08.310
"Maybe you should consider

00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:10.169
applying to the
engineering school."

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.300
I was applying to Princeton.

00:01:11.301 --> 00:01:14.000
I said, "Why not?"
I checked the box.

00:01:14.001 --> 00:01:19.150
So, I started in
Computer Science.

00:01:19.151 --> 00:01:21.000
At that time, it was electrical

00:01:21.001 --> 00:01:23.390
engineering and Computer Science.

00:01:23.391 --> 00:01:25.980
I pretty much liked
my first year.

00:01:25.981 --> 00:01:28.200
I started the circuits course

00:01:28.201 --> 00:01:30.510
in my second year and
I didn't like it,

00:01:30.511 --> 00:01:32.849
and I switched out of
the School of Engineering

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.315
and wouldn't come back until

00:01:34.316 --> 00:01:40.425
they waived the requirement
for the circuits course.

00:01:40.426 --> 00:01:43.805
Then, in junior year,

00:01:43.806 --> 00:01:45.830
I met formal languages in

00:01:45.831 --> 00:01:48.470
Automata theory and
I just fell in love.

00:01:48.471 --> 00:01:50.130
When I was ready to graduate,

00:01:50.131 --> 00:01:51.900
I decided I wanted to continue

00:01:51.901 --> 00:01:56.645
my studies and I went to
Cornell and studied theory.

00:01:56.646 --> 00:01:59.970
My first work was in
distributed computing.

00:01:59.971 --> 00:02:01.790
I did that for a number of years.

00:02:01.791 --> 00:02:05.100
I was heavily influenced
by Nancy Lynch,

00:02:05.101 --> 00:02:06.720
who had come from MIT to give

00:02:06.721 --> 00:02:09.855
a talk when I was
a graduate student at Cornell.

00:02:09.856 --> 00:02:12.610
I postdoced for her.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:18.750
At a certain point,

00:02:18.751 --> 00:02:22.835
we needed, well, so in
distributed computing,

00:02:22.836 --> 00:02:26.230
you're often talking about
good guys computing in

00:02:26.231 --> 00:02:27.800
the presence of
some bad guys that are

00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:29.795
trying to disrupt
the computation.

00:02:29.796 --> 00:02:32.050
It's very natural
that you might use

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:35.950
cryptography to hide
information from the bad guys.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.540
What we discovered was that the

00:02:39.541 --> 00:02:42.070
kinds of cryptography
that existed,

00:02:42.071 --> 00:02:45.640
the cryptography that captured

00:02:45.641 --> 00:02:48.800
privacy from
a passive eavesdropper,

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:51.385
when messages are
going back and forth,

00:02:51.386 --> 00:02:54.305
is not the same as
what you needed when

00:02:54.306 --> 00:02:56.050
the adversary is playing

00:02:56.051 --> 00:02:57.970
the same game as all
of the good guys.

00:02:57.971 --> 00:03:01.860
So, we needed basically a
different kind of cryptography.

00:03:01.861 --> 00:03:07.055
So, I ended up studying
cryptography and I got into,

00:03:07.056 --> 00:03:10.130
developed something called
Non-Malleable Cryptography,

00:03:10.131 --> 00:03:13.105
together with Moni Naor
and Danny Dolev.

00:03:13.106 --> 00:03:15.970
Then I loved crypto,

00:03:15.971 --> 00:03:19.590
and I did that for
quite some time,.

00:03:19.591 --> 00:03:24.330
Eventually, around the year 2000,

00:03:24.331 --> 00:03:29.080
I was talking to the philosopher
Helen Nissenbaum,

00:03:29.081 --> 00:03:31.300
who studies philosophical issues

00:03:31.301 --> 00:03:34.080
that arise in the context
of new technologies.

00:03:34.081 --> 00:03:36.340
Nissenbaum was studying what does

00:03:36.341 --> 00:03:38.530
privacy mean in public and what

00:03:38.531 --> 00:03:40.330
does privacy mean when we

00:03:40.331 --> 00:03:43.850
have video cameras
all over the place?

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.800
I got very intrigued.

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:50.895
I looked for a piece of
the privacy puzzle that

00:03:50.896 --> 00:03:53.530
theoretical Computer Science
and cryptography might

00:03:53.531 --> 00:03:56.525
be able to say something about.

00:03:56.526 --> 00:04:00.775
Crypto had devolved a lot of
language for talking about

00:04:00.776 --> 00:04:03.340
partial information and that

00:04:03.341 --> 00:04:05.690
seemed like a good beginning,

00:04:05.691 --> 00:04:09.685
and that is how I
got into privacy

00:04:09.686 --> 00:04:13.735
and led to differential
privacy eventually.

00:04:13.736 --> 00:04:17.150
&gt;&gt; That's fascinating.
So these topics that

00:04:17.151 --> 00:04:18.610
you've been working on

00:04:18.611 --> 00:04:21.650
from both privacy and
then now fairness,

00:04:21.651 --> 00:04:24.870
these are age old
problems, right?

00:04:24.871 --> 00:04:28.370
That philosophers, ethicists,
and the legal scholars

00:04:28.371 --> 00:04:32.330
have been thinking
about for centuries,

00:04:32.331 --> 00:04:33.670
and now you've brought,

00:04:33.671 --> 00:04:36.100
I think a very new perspective,

00:04:36.101 --> 00:04:38.755
the Computer Science, a
theoretical perspective.

00:04:38.756 --> 00:04:43.685
I'm curious, since
you've been doing that,

00:04:43.686 --> 00:04:46.480
how has that
perspective, you think,

00:04:46.481 --> 00:04:50.220
shaped or affected the broader
discussion about privacy

00:04:50.221 --> 00:04:54.695
and now how people are
thinking about fairness?

00:04:54.696 --> 00:05:01.450
&gt;&gt; So I don't think that
what we've done in privacy

00:05:01.451 --> 00:05:03.955
preserving data analysis really

00:05:03.956 --> 00:05:07.720
addresses large questions about

00:05:07.721 --> 00:05:09.745
how people feel about privacy.

00:05:09.746 --> 00:05:13.030
It was a very specific corner

00:05:13.031 --> 00:05:16.370
of the general privacy puzzle.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.140
We did look a bit to

00:05:20.141 --> 00:05:22.540
the legal and the
philosophical literature,

00:05:22.541 --> 00:05:29.070
to help us try to articulate
specific privacy concerns,

00:05:29.071 --> 00:05:34.060
meaning legal scholars and
philosophers will try to find

00:05:34.061 --> 00:05:36.340
very precise language to

00:05:36.341 --> 00:05:38.980
capture things that people

00:05:38.981 --> 00:05:41.290
feel and why they
feel the way they do.

00:05:41.291 --> 00:05:47.250
So, we got help from them
more than vice versa.

00:05:47.251 --> 00:05:51.684
In the fairness domain,

00:05:51.685 --> 00:05:55.980
I think that there is

00:05:55.981 --> 00:06:01.600
some chance that
the data analytic tools

00:06:01.601 --> 00:06:05.790
can help shine spotlight on how

00:06:05.791 --> 00:06:11.690
people think and where there
are unfairnesses and biases.

00:06:11.691 --> 00:06:16.590
So, we may get

00:06:16.591 --> 00:06:20.980
some help on the bigger
questions from the technology.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.160
So, I love that,

00:06:23.161 --> 00:06:25.530
you've mentioned
the interplay between

00:06:25.531 --> 00:06:27.510
Computer Science and
other disciplines

00:06:27.511 --> 00:06:28.980
and borrowing ideas from them,

00:06:28.981 --> 00:06:30.720
and now, perhaps feeding back a

00:06:30.721 --> 00:06:32.550
little as well and trying to

00:06:32.551 --> 00:06:36.190
figure out the solutions to
these challenges together.

00:06:36.191 --> 00:06:38.790
How do you go about engaging with

00:06:38.791 --> 00:06:42.495
people outside of
our home discipline?

00:06:42.496 --> 00:06:49.000
How do I go about it?
So, I guess research,

00:06:49.001 --> 00:06:51.300
like any other kind of research.

00:06:51.301 --> 00:06:55.795
So, usually there's
some sort of starting point,

00:06:55.796 --> 00:06:57.890
like in the privacy domain,

00:06:57.891 --> 00:07:02.140
my interest was
piqued by Nissenbaum.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:07.205
In other areas in fairness,

00:07:07.206 --> 00:07:11.480
I could start
immediately looking for

00:07:11.481 --> 00:07:15.610
people who think about questions
of distributive justice,

00:07:15.611 --> 00:07:19.390
who think about gender
bias in medicine,

00:07:19.391 --> 00:07:25.600
who think about racial bias.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:32.560
When you have some successful
conversations with people,

00:07:32.561 --> 00:07:35.950
they're kind and refer
you to their friends.

00:07:35.951 --> 00:07:38.750
So, I've been tremendously
lucky and had

00:07:38.751 --> 00:07:41.785
the opportunity to talk to
a lot of people this way.

00:07:41.786 --> 00:07:42.930
So, networking I guess,

00:07:42.931 --> 00:07:44.315
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:07:44.316 --> 00:07:47.040
&gt;&gt; -and reading.
A lot of reading.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:50.865
&gt;&gt; Do you think
Computer Science as a whole,

00:07:50.866 --> 00:07:53.820
does enough of this type
of engagement or just

00:07:53.821 --> 00:07:57.360
the right amount? I'm curious.

00:07:57.361 --> 00:08:04.939
&gt;&gt; So I think that
Computer Scientists,

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:07.390
more than ever, have
to start thinking

00:08:07.391 --> 00:08:10.125
about ethics, and being
educated about ethics.

00:08:10.126 --> 00:08:13.120
That Computer Science is

00:08:13.121 --> 00:08:16.145
moving into a position
of tremendous power,

00:08:16.146 --> 00:08:20.260
and with that comes
ethical responsibilities.

00:08:20.261 --> 00:08:22.070
At the same time,

00:08:22.071 --> 00:08:27.210
everybody has his or her own
strengths and interests,

00:08:27.211 --> 00:08:29.390
and for the people who only

00:08:29.391 --> 00:08:31.980
want to study one
kind of thing deeply,

00:08:31.981 --> 00:08:34.590
I certainly wouldn't
want to derail them.

00:08:34.591 --> 00:08:37.470
So, I think naturally,

00:08:37.471 --> 00:08:39.020
we're going to have a big mix.

00:08:39.021 --> 00:08:41.330
But I do feel that students

00:08:41.331 --> 00:08:44.630
should be educated about ethics.

00:08:44.631 --> 00:08:47.350
Harvard is moving in
that direction in

00:08:47.351 --> 00:08:50.075
Computer Science as well.

00:08:50.076 --> 00:08:52.560
So, I had an ethicist

00:08:52.561 --> 00:08:55.045
come and speak and
work with the class,

00:08:55.046 --> 00:08:57.495
in the class that I
taught most recently,

00:08:57.496 --> 00:09:00.250
and several other people
are doing this.

00:09:00.251 --> 00:09:03.810
&gt;&gt; When we were talking
earlier this morning,

00:09:03.811 --> 00:09:05.660
you mentioned that you
thought that the work you're

00:09:05.661 --> 00:09:07.880
doing now in bias

00:09:07.881 --> 00:09:10.170
is a lot more
challenging in some ways

00:09:10.171 --> 00:09:12.525
than your earlier work
on differential privacy.

00:09:12.526 --> 00:09:13.700
I'm curious if you

00:09:13.701 --> 00:09:15.615
could say a little
bit more about that.

00:09:15.616 --> 00:09:20.130
&gt;&gt; Sure. So, I

00:09:20.131 --> 00:09:25.055
think that defining bias
is much, much tougher.

00:09:25.056 --> 00:09:26.810
So, the first thing
that you want to do,

00:09:26.811 --> 00:09:29.190
if you want to have
a technical solution

00:09:29.191 --> 00:09:33.140
that helps with at least some
aspect of a bias problem,

00:09:33.141 --> 00:09:34.650
is to define what bias is,

00:09:34.651 --> 00:09:38.750
or to define what is the
unfairness that you're trying

00:09:38.751 --> 00:09:41.590
to combat in
your particular corner

00:09:41.591 --> 00:09:43.870
of the technological universe.

00:09:43.871 --> 00:09:48.170
So, when we were talking about

00:09:48.171 --> 00:09:50.850
privacy and we had narrowed it

00:09:50.851 --> 00:09:53.670
down to privacy preserving
data analysis,

00:09:53.671 --> 00:09:57.470
which is a much smaller scope
than privacy in general.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:02.425
We could think about different
definitions of privacy,

00:10:02.426 --> 00:10:09.310
and we came up with one
which is rather robust,

00:10:09.311 --> 00:10:12.425
and it captures many intuitions

00:10:12.426 --> 00:10:16.750
about what constitutes
privacy in data analysis.

00:10:16.751 --> 00:10:19.290
So, you come up with it,

00:10:19.291 --> 00:10:21.150
you develop methods
that insure it,

00:10:21.151 --> 00:10:23.840
and there's not controversy
about what you're doing.

00:10:23.841 --> 00:10:25.680
It may or may not do everything,

00:10:25.681 --> 00:10:27.820
but it does what it says it does,

00:10:27.821 --> 00:10:30.350
and people don't argue that

00:10:30.351 --> 00:10:33.900
what it says it's
doing is a bad thing,

00:10:33.901 --> 00:10:35.345
it's only a good thing.

00:10:35.346 --> 00:10:38.090
Now, when it comes to fairness,

00:10:38.091 --> 00:10:40.630
things go all over the place.

00:10:40.631 --> 00:10:42.900
So, for example, you
could say, "Well,

00:10:42.901 --> 00:10:46.780
I want to have a classifier and I

00:10:46.781 --> 00:10:53.290
want to ensure what we
called demographic parity."

00:10:53.291 --> 00:10:55.605
That is the number,
the fraction of

00:10:55.606 --> 00:11:00.090
people in a given demographic
group that are, let's say,

00:11:00.091 --> 00:11:02.385
receiving a positive
classification,

00:11:02.386 --> 00:11:04.160
should be the same
as this fraction of

00:11:04.161 --> 00:11:08.235
the demographic group in
the population as a whole.

00:11:08.236 --> 00:11:10.995
Is that fair or isn't it?

00:11:10.996 --> 00:11:14.665
Is affirmative action
fair or isn't it?

00:11:14.666 --> 00:11:18.140
If you are looking

00:11:18.141 --> 00:11:23.050
at cases where you're
trying to make predictions,

00:11:23.051 --> 00:11:24.410
and you're going
to have some error

00:11:24.411 --> 00:11:27.270
because the future
is not knowable,

00:11:27.271 --> 00:11:31.690
do you require
equal false positive rates

00:11:31.691 --> 00:11:34.740
and false negative rates
across groups or not?

00:11:34.741 --> 00:11:37.230
What are the consequences
of these things?

00:11:37.231 --> 00:11:42.955
So, a lot of natural definitions,

00:11:42.956 --> 00:11:46.240
natural desiderata, are
mutually exclusive.

00:11:46.241 --> 00:11:48.600
What do you do about that?

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:51.780
What can you ask
your system to do when

00:11:51.781 --> 00:11:52.940
these various things that you

00:11:52.941 --> 00:11:54.850
want are mutually exclusive?

00:11:54.851 --> 00:11:56.350
You have to come to some kind of

00:11:56.351 --> 00:11:59.385
a social agreement as to
what you mean by fair,

00:11:59.386 --> 00:12:01.140
and what's appropriate
for your setting.

00:12:01.141 --> 00:12:03.790
I'm starting to understand

00:12:03.791 --> 00:12:07.170
that this is a fundamentally
political process.

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:11.350
My politics education
is just beginning.

00:12:11.351 --> 00:12:12.970
&gt;&gt; So do you think the role

00:12:12.971 --> 00:12:17.210
of the language and
framework theory

00:12:17.211 --> 00:12:19.980
behind fairness is about

00:12:19.981 --> 00:12:24.075
showing the options to
policymakers and legal folks,

00:12:24.076 --> 00:12:25.835
or what role do you see?

00:12:25.836 --> 00:12:28.235
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, first of all,

00:12:28.236 --> 00:12:31.515
I should mention there is
a definition that I like,

00:12:31.516 --> 00:12:34.255
although it too has
all sorts of problems.

00:12:34.256 --> 00:12:36.150
The definition that
I like is what

00:12:36.151 --> 00:12:38.310
we're calling,
individual fairness.

00:12:38.311 --> 00:12:40.395
Which says intuitively that,

00:12:40.396 --> 00:12:43.895
if given a particular
classification task,

00:12:43.896 --> 00:12:46.960
individuals who are
similar with respect to

00:12:46.961 --> 00:12:50.540
that classification task should
be classified similarly,

00:12:50.541 --> 00:12:52.500
should have similar
probabilities of

00:12:52.501 --> 00:12:55.820
positive classification,
for example.

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:59.800
Of course, the problem
with that is, well,

00:12:59.801 --> 00:13:04.180
how do we determine who is
similar to whom for this task?

00:13:04.181 --> 00:13:05.525
What is the metric?

00:13:05.526 --> 00:13:07.330
What is the measure
of distance of

00:13:07.331 --> 00:13:11.385
similarity or dissimilarity
between two people?

00:13:11.386 --> 00:13:16.050
Even if you had
your hands on a metric,

00:13:16.051 --> 00:13:18.150
how do you know that
it's the right one?

00:13:18.151 --> 00:13:20.530
So, an example that
I like to think

00:13:20.531 --> 00:13:23.655
about is, let's say that,

00:13:23.656 --> 00:13:27.915
we have a very nice
news organization

00:13:27.916 --> 00:13:30.010
and we are looking to

00:13:30.011 --> 00:13:32.335
hire people to
our news organization.

00:13:32.336 --> 00:13:34.630
So, we want to
predict who is likely

00:13:34.631 --> 00:13:37.130
to succeed at
the news organization,

00:13:37.131 --> 00:13:39.800
and we have a very crisp
definition of success.

00:13:39.801 --> 00:13:42.210
You stay three years with
the company at least,

00:13:42.211 --> 00:13:44.925
and you have at least one
promotion in those three years.

00:13:44.926 --> 00:13:47.215
So, success is well-defined.

00:13:47.216 --> 00:13:49.690
So, then you want
to say, somehow,

00:13:49.691 --> 00:13:51.160
that if you have

00:13:51.161 --> 00:13:53.875
two people who are similarly
likely to succeed,

00:13:53.876 --> 00:13:56.540
then they should have
the same probabilities

00:13:56.541 --> 00:13:58.050
of getting a job offer,

00:13:58.051 --> 00:14:00.350
or similar probabilities
of getting the job offer.

00:14:00.351 --> 00:14:06.780
So now let's change
the problem and we say, well,

00:14:06.781 --> 00:14:09.260
maybe it's not such a
nice news organization,

00:14:09.261 --> 00:14:11.300
maybe it's a news
organization that

00:14:11.301 --> 00:14:14.210
has historically been
hostile to women.

00:14:14.211 --> 00:14:20.850
So now, for two equally
talented individuals,

00:14:20.851 --> 00:14:22.360
one male and one female,

00:14:22.361 --> 00:14:24.590
it may be that the man
is more likely to

00:14:24.591 --> 00:14:28.070
succeed than the woman because
the atmosphere is hostile.

00:14:28.071 --> 00:14:30.680
So, wait, should the metric be

00:14:30.681 --> 00:14:33.820
capturing likelihood of success

00:14:33.821 --> 00:14:36.405
or should the metric
in fact be talent?

00:14:36.406 --> 00:14:39.000
In the nice news organization,

00:14:39.001 --> 00:14:41.070
talent predicts your success.

00:14:41.071 --> 00:14:42.905
In the nasty news organization,

00:14:42.906 --> 00:14:45.580
talent is insufficient
to predict success.

00:14:45.581 --> 00:14:49.650
So, you need to
exercise thought and

00:14:49.651 --> 00:14:51.090
judgment about what should

00:14:51.091 --> 00:14:53.860
the classifiers be doing
and how do you use them?

00:14:53.861 --> 00:14:55.585
On the other hand,

00:14:55.586 --> 00:14:59.040
when you actually
formalize the problem,

00:14:59.041 --> 00:15:01.080
it becomes abundantly clear that

00:15:01.081 --> 00:15:03.345
the issue is hostility
in the work environment.

00:15:03.346 --> 00:15:04.760
So, there's something
that's gained

00:15:04.761 --> 00:15:07.855
immediately from the
formalization of the problem.

00:15:07.856 --> 00:15:13.840
&gt;&gt; So you brought up the issue
of these algorithms,

00:15:13.841 --> 00:15:16.850
accidentally learning from

00:15:16.851 --> 00:15:20.150
society's biases and
amplifying them,

00:15:20.151 --> 00:15:23.170
potentially in the decisions
that they make.

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:25.620
That's one source of bias

00:15:25.621 --> 00:15:26.830
I hear people talking
about but might

00:15:26.831 --> 00:15:29.840
also hear people talking
about data biases,

00:15:29.841 --> 00:15:32.710
where the data that's used
to train an algorithm is

00:15:32.711 --> 00:15:34.870
perhaps an insufficient
representation

00:15:34.871 --> 00:15:36.565
of what goes in the world.

00:15:36.566 --> 00:15:38.150
Maybe it's bias through

00:15:38.151 --> 00:15:41.640
some selection mechanism
or collection mechanism.

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:44.870
Do you see these
two types of biases

00:15:44.871 --> 00:15:47.950
as two sides of the same coin

00:15:47.951 --> 00:15:50.610
or do you think that they
are different and require

00:15:50.611 --> 00:15:53.755
different approaches to address?

00:15:53.756 --> 00:15:55.370
&gt;&gt; So I'm not actually
clear what you

00:15:55.371 --> 00:15:57.130
mean in your first example.

00:15:57.131 --> 00:15:59.200
In fact, when you were
saying your first example,

00:15:59.201 --> 00:16:00.880
I thought that you
meant the second.

00:16:00.881 --> 00:16:04.380
That is, that the biases

00:16:04.381 --> 00:16:07.490
are imbibed in the mother's milk
of the training data.

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:11.340
Then, what's learned,

00:16:11.341 --> 00:16:13.040
it's not an accident
that it's learned.

00:16:13.041 --> 00:16:14.640
It wasn't the intention of

00:16:14.641 --> 00:16:17.940
the algorithm designer to teach

00:16:17.941 --> 00:16:20.370
the algorithm to be
biased but the biases are

00:16:20.371 --> 00:16:24.070
just brought in there from
the historical training data.

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:27.740
&gt;&gt; So I was thinking
about the two kinds

00:16:27.741 --> 00:16:30.440
of biases, I was thinking about,

00:16:30.441 --> 00:16:33.445
was one where there is
an existing bias in

00:16:33.446 --> 00:16:37.650
an organization like
the company versus another one

00:16:37.651 --> 00:16:39.320
where the organization
is not biased but

00:16:39.321 --> 00:16:43.435
perhaps the data that
you collected about

00:16:43.436 --> 00:16:47.050
employee success was selected in

00:16:47.051 --> 00:16:50.930
such a way to not be representative
of that organization.

00:16:50.931 --> 00:16:53.720
&gt;&gt; So clearly, a biased
organization is giving

00:16:53.721 --> 00:16:57.935
rise to what I would call
biased historical data.

00:16:57.936 --> 00:17:00.640
So probably, if I'm

00:17:00.641 --> 00:17:03.110
interpreting your question
correctly, the answer is yes.

00:17:03.111 --> 00:17:05.730
These are two sides
of the same coin.

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:09.805
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I guess in
one case you have

00:17:09.806 --> 00:17:11.620
opportunities to go and collect

00:17:11.621 --> 00:17:13.075
more data and the other one

00:17:13.076 --> 00:17:14.990
you can't without
fixing others section.

00:17:14.991 --> 00:17:16.740
&gt;&gt; So you're alluding
to something else which

00:17:16.741 --> 00:17:19.530
is just lack of representation.

00:17:19.531 --> 00:17:21.920
Lack of representation presents

00:17:21.921 --> 00:17:23.000
its own problems and

00:17:23.001 --> 00:17:26.080
historically has
presented bad problems.

00:17:26.081 --> 00:17:29.765
One can collect more data
and one can also

00:17:29.766 --> 00:17:33.820
be smarter about
exploration-exploitation trade-offs.

00:17:33.821 --> 00:17:36.000
So, there's starting to be

00:17:36.001 --> 00:17:38.315
a nice literature on this thing

00:17:38.316 --> 00:17:43.665
in the fair algorithms world.

00:17:43.666 --> 00:17:45.395
But I think also that,

00:17:45.396 --> 00:17:46.740
if I'm not mistaken,

00:17:46.741 --> 00:17:48.580
economists were
also aware of this.

00:17:48.581 --> 00:17:50.169
So, you have two populations,

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:53.370
a minority population and
a majority population.

00:17:53.371 --> 00:17:55.470
You know the majority very well.

00:17:55.471 --> 00:17:57.450
What kinds of incentives can you

00:17:57.451 --> 00:17:59.380
offer a bank for giving loans to

00:17:59.381 --> 00:18:01.145
the minority populations so

00:18:01.146 --> 00:18:04.095
that population
becomes better known.

00:18:04.096 --> 00:18:08.500
So, these questions
are not entirely new.

00:18:08.501 --> 00:18:09.670
These questions of lack of

00:18:09.671 --> 00:18:12.500
representation are not
entirely new and I think

00:18:12.501 --> 00:18:14.320
we should be thinking
a lot in terms

00:18:14.321 --> 00:18:19.540
of what sorts of things
can we do to encourage

00:18:19.541 --> 00:18:27.765
the increasing of the data sets
and balancing out,

00:18:27.766 --> 00:18:30.005
so that we get
much more informed views.

00:18:30.006 --> 00:18:33.530
How do we bring in experts on

00:18:33.531 --> 00:18:36.230
different populations
and include them in

00:18:36.231 --> 00:18:39.465
the discussion of what
constitutes fair,

00:18:39.466 --> 00:18:41.540
and what are
the right criteria to

00:18:41.541 --> 00:18:44.830
be using for
the different groups?

00:18:44.831 --> 00:18:51.170
I know that there is
some interest in having,

00:18:51.171 --> 00:18:53.900
say classifiers that suppressed

00:18:53.901 --> 00:18:56.525
the information of
the demographic group.

00:18:56.526 --> 00:18:59.250
My guess is, well not my guess,

00:18:59.251 --> 00:19:02.100
we know specifically that

00:19:02.101 --> 00:19:04.430
there are situations
in which doing

00:19:04.431 --> 00:19:09.370
this leads to reduced utility

00:19:09.371 --> 00:19:15.550
that having context allows
you to make better judgments.

00:19:15.551 --> 00:19:19.770
&gt;&gt; Where do you see
the future lies in fairness?

00:19:19.771 --> 00:19:22.620
Do you think that will

00:19:22.621 --> 00:19:26.210
make significant headway
in the next few years from

00:19:26.211 --> 00:19:27.760
either practical or theoretical

00:19:27.761 --> 00:19:28.810
point of view or do you think

00:19:28.811 --> 00:19:33.715
that it's a long ways off yet?

00:19:33.716 --> 00:19:36.500
Given the politics and

00:19:36.501 --> 00:19:39.650
the social aspects of
challenges or the problem.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:46.605
&gt;&gt; I think real progress on

00:19:46.606 --> 00:19:52.700
the mathematical side
will be relatively rapid.

00:19:52.701 --> 00:19:56.395
That I'm seeing just in
the last couple of years,

00:19:56.396 --> 00:20:02.440
a real gathering of strength
and a plethora of new ideas

00:20:02.441 --> 00:20:06.890
and variations on the definitions

00:20:06.891 --> 00:20:09.210
that try to bridge the individual

00:20:09.211 --> 00:20:12.705
versus the group in
very exciting ways.

00:20:12.706 --> 00:20:20.710
There are also some extremely
intriguing approaches

00:20:20.711 --> 00:20:23.200
coming up in the deep
learning community.

00:20:23.201 --> 00:20:29.400
So, I think that this
is going really well.

00:20:29.401 --> 00:20:32.550
I don't believe there will be

00:20:32.551 --> 00:20:39.040
an alternative to
having discussions that

00:20:39.041 --> 00:20:43.350
bring in politics and ethics

00:20:43.351 --> 00:20:49.040
and economics and so on.

00:20:49.041 --> 00:20:51.180
I think that the charges to

00:20:51.181 --> 00:20:53.300
these communities are going

00:20:53.301 --> 00:20:54.825
to go up and you're going to say,

00:20:54.826 --> 00:20:57.405
we need help with this
that and the other thing.

00:20:57.406 --> 00:21:02.215
So, I think things
will make progress,

00:21:02.216 --> 00:21:07.730
but I don't see

00:21:07.731 --> 00:21:09.810
yet our way to finding

00:21:09.811 --> 00:21:12.985
a single crisp definition
of what fair is,

00:21:12.986 --> 00:21:16.180
that will really capture
what we want all the

00:21:16.181 --> 00:21:19.435
time from an algorithm,

00:21:19.436 --> 00:21:22.320
a fair algorithm, and that will

00:21:22.321 --> 00:21:25.435
behave very well under
what we call composition.

00:21:25.436 --> 00:21:27.250
We haven't spent any time talking

00:21:27.251 --> 00:21:30.550
about these very
important questions of,

00:21:30.551 --> 00:21:32.550
if you have a system
that has a bunch

00:21:32.551 --> 00:21:34.580
of pieces each of which is fair,

00:21:34.581 --> 00:21:37.640
what can you say about fairness

00:21:37.641 --> 00:21:41.265
of your system in
total altogether?

00:21:41.266 --> 00:21:43.820
In other scenarios, in

00:21:43.821 --> 00:21:47.040
cryptography and in
differential privacy people

00:21:47.041 --> 00:21:48.320
know how to talk about

00:21:48.321 --> 00:21:52.835
composition and ensure
nice composition properties.

00:21:52.836 --> 00:21:54.760
But in the fairness domain,

00:21:54.761 --> 00:21:56.770
things are much more
difficult and we

00:21:56.771 --> 00:21:59.150
get initial results are
very negative and we

00:21:59.151 --> 00:22:04.435
have to work hard to
ensure fairness together.

00:22:04.436 --> 00:22:08.095
&gt;&gt; That's good to hear.

00:22:08.096 --> 00:22:10.400
I think we're out of time

00:22:10.401 --> 00:22:12.235
and your talk is to
be coming up soon.

00:22:12.236 --> 00:22:13.650
Thank you very much
Cynthia for coming.

00:22:13.651 --> 00:22:15.030
It's been a pleasure
talking with you.

00:22:15.031 --> 00:22:17.960
The pleasure it's been
mine. Thank you so much.

